    "outline": [
      "PHASE 1 - PRE-PANDEMIC (pre-2020): Public health: routine care, no universal masking; WHO declared COVID-19 PHEIC Jan 30, 2020. Work: offices dominant, ~5% remote (US BLS est.); Zoom IPO Apr 2019. Education: in-person classrooms; UNESCO ~1.6B learners affected later. Travel: record ~4.5B air passengers 2019 (IATA est.); no PCR tests. Economy: global GDP growth ~2.8% 2019 (IMF est.); low debt. Mental health: stigma, limited telehealth. Social: handshakes, crowded events. Tech: cloud/5G early, e-commerce ~15% of retail (US est.).",
      "PHASE 2 - EARLY 2020 OUTBREAK (Jan-Mar 2020): Public health: Wuhan lockdown Jan 23, 2020; WHO PHEIC Jan 30; pandemic declared Mar 11. Work: mass WFH; Zoom daily users ~300M by Apr 2020 (company claim). Education: school closures; China/Italy first; UNESCO ~1.6B learners (est.). Travel: border closures; IATA ~95% drop in international traffic Apr 2020 (est.). Economy: US CARES Act Mar 2020 (~$2.2T); UK furlough; global GDP -3.1% 2020 (IMF est.). Mental health: anxiety/depression spikes; WHO surveys. Social: social distancing, masks. Tech: Zoom/Teams surge; contact tracing apps (e.g., Singapore TraceTogether).",
      "PHASE 3 - GLOBAL RESPONSE (2020-2021): Public health: lockdowns (UK Mar 2020, Italy, India); masks; testing; WHO COVAX launched Apr 2020. Work: hybrid pilots; Twitter announced permanent WFH May 2020. Education: remote learning; learning loss estimates (World Bank ~0.5-1 SD, est.). Travel: EU travel bans; IATA ~1.8B passengers 2020 (est.). Economy: stimulus (US $1.9T ARP Mar 2021); supply chain disruptions; inflation rise. Mental health: WHO loneliness report; telehealth expansion. Social: Zoom holidays; social isolation. Tech: e-commerce surge; Netflix ~200M subs 2020; remote work tools.",
      "PHASE 4 - VACCINE ROLLOUT (Dec 2020-2022): Public health: Pfizer-BioNTech/Moderna EUA Dec 2020; ~13B doses administered globally by mid-2022 (WHO est.); boosters. Work: return-to-office debates; hybrid norm; 'Great Resignation' (US quits ~4M/month 2021-22, BLS). Education: reopening with masks; learning recovery programs. Travel: vaccine passports (EU Digital COVID Certificate Jul 2021); IATA ~2.3B passengers 2021 (est.). Economy: reopening boom; inflation ~7-9% US 2022 (BLS); supply chain easing. Mental health: mixed; burnout. Social: reconnection; vaccine mandates. Tech: mRNA platform; AI/automation acceleration.",
      "PHASE 5 - 2025 POST-PANDEMIC LIFESTYLE: Public health: COVID endemic; WHO ended PHEIC May 2023; routine vaccination; long COVID (~10-20% of infected, est.). Work: hybrid ~30% of US days remote (est.); office vacancy ~20% US (est.); AI tools. Education: hybrid learning; digital literacy; learning loss persists. Travel: ~4.5B passengers 2024 (IATA est., near 2019); digital health passes. Economy: AI-driven productivity; remote work tax implications; debt high. Mental health: telehealth mainstream; youth mental health crisis. Social: digital-first socializing; reduced handshakes. Tech: AI (ChatGPT Nov 2022), 5G, cloud; e-commerce ~20%+ retail (est.).",
      "BEFORE/AFTER CONTRASTS (summary): Public health: reactive to proactive surveillance; Work: office-centric to hybrid/remote; Education: in-person to blended; Travel: unrestricted to health-checked; Economy: growth to volatility then AI-driven; Mental health: stigmatized to telehealth-normalized; Social: physical to digital-hybrid; Technology: incremental to accelerated adoption.",
      "UNCERTAINTY FLAGS: All figures are model general knowledge approximations; no external retrieval performed. Specific numbers (e.g., IATA passenger counts, IMF GDP, BLS quits) are estimates from memory and may vary by source/date. Long COVID prevalence and 2025 trends are projections, not confirmed data.",
      "KEY EXAMPLES: WHO (PHEIC/pandemic declarations), Zoom (WFH surge), UNESCO (school closures), IATA (travel collapse), US CARES Act/ARP (stimulus), UK furlough, Twitter (permanent WFH), EU Digital COVID Certificate (vaccine passports), Pfizer/Moderna (mRNA), ChatGPT (AI acceleration), Netflix (streaming growth)."
    ],
